  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\n\nLight rain continues to spread eastwards into western \nWisconsin, but should begin to diminish as we get later into the\nafternoon. Rainfall amounts will be on the order of a tenth of \nan inch or less. Skies remain mostly cloudy into Friday which \nwill keep temperatures fairly steady, falling to around 60 \novernight & warming back into the upper 60s tomorrow afternoon. \n\nRain chances return this weekend as a weak disturbance moves \nthrough the Upper Midwest. Saturday currently looks like the \nwetter day, with scattered showers & a few thunderstorms developing\nacross much of Minnesota & western Wisconsin. Similar to today,\nshowers will be more widespread across western Minnesota \ncompared to eastern Minnesota & western Wisconsin. Expected \nrainfall amounts this weekend range from around half an inch \nacross western Minnesota to only a tenth or less across western \nWisconsin.\n\nA stronger shortwave & surface front approaches the region\nMonday. Southerly winds ahead of this system should allow \ntemperatures to moderate somewhat, with highs climbing back into\nthe upper 60s to around 70 in many areas. Increasing moisture \nwill also set the stage for a more widespread round of rain as a\ncold front moves through Monday night into Tuesday. This \ncurrently looks like the best chance for a broad, soaking rain \nduring the next several days, with current guidance supporting a\nwidespread 0.50-1\" of rain and locally higher amounts possible.\nSevere weather still appears unlikely, but embedded \nthunderstorms could enhance rainfall rates in spots.\n\nCooler air filters back into the region behind the front Tuesday into\nWednesday, with more fall-like temperatures through the rest of\nthe week with highs in the low to mid 60s & lows in the 40s. \nThe pattern may remain active as another disturbance develops \nalong or north of the stalled frontal zone late Wednesday into \nThursday. Some spread in the guidance exists on just how far\nnorth the precipitation fro this system may extend into\nMinnesota, but at least the far-souther portion of the state\nappears positioned for another round of appreciable rainfall.\nOtherwise, high pressure building over the Upper Midwest should\nkeep us cool & dry through next weekend.",
